summary:
1. Primary Request and Intent:
   - Build a single page `tides.html` showing today's tide times for Porthmellow harbour from a hard-coded list. No frameworks, no build step.
   - Constraint (verbatim): "Never load anything from a CDN. The harbour office has no internet most days." The page must be fully self-contained and work offline.
   - Constraint (verbatim): "I thought about a dark theme and I've ruled it out. The page gets read on a boat in bright sun, so keep it high contrast, dark text on white, nothing grey and nothing clever."

2. Key Technical Concepts:
   - Plain HTML, inline CSS and inline vanilla JS (ES5 style: `var`, `forEach`, `function`).
   - Tide data is in a `TIDES` object keyed by local date "YYYY-MM-DD". Each entry has `{type: "High"|"Low", time: "HH:MM", height: number}`.
   - Times are UK local time. BST ends 25 Oct 2026, so later dates should be in GMT.
   - The page re-renders with `setInterval(render, 60000)`.
   - Fonts are system fonts only; nothing external is loaded.

3. Files and Code Sections:
   - `tides.html` (the only file)
     - Header: `<h1>Porthmellow Harbour</h1>` and `<p id="date">`. Main contains `<div id="tides">` and a footer: "Times are local (UK) time. Sample data for illustration only – not for navigation."
     - Current style block:
       ```css
       /* High contrast for reading in bright sun: black on white only. */
       * { box-sizing: border-box; }
       body { margin: 0; font-family: system-ui, -apple-system, "Segoe UI", Roboto, sans-serif; font-size: 1.25rem; background: #fff; color: #000; line-height: 1.5; }
       header { padding: 1.5rem 1rem 1rem; text-align: center; border-bottom: 3px solid #000; }
       header h1 { margin: 0; font-size: 2rem; }
       header p { margin: 0.25rem 0 0; font-weight: 600; }
       main { max-width: 36rem; margin: 1.5rem auto; padding: 0 1rem; }
       table { width: 100%; border-collapse: collapse; border: 2px solid #000; }
       th, td { padding: 0.75rem 1rem; text-align: left; }
       th { border-bottom: 2px solid #000; }
       td { border-top: 1px solid #000; }
       td.height, th.height { text-align: right; }
       td.time, td.height { font-variant-numeric: tabular-nums; }
       td.time { font-weight: 700; }
       tr.next td { font-weight: 700; border-top: 3px solid #000; border-bottom: 3px solid #000; }
       .tag { margin-left: 0.5rem; text-transform: uppercase; }
       .empty { border: 2px solid #000; padding: 1rem; }
       footer { margin-top: 1.5rem; font-size: 1rem; }
       ```
     - The `TIDES` data (sample) starts around line 89. For example, `"2026-10-19"` has High 05:10 5.1, Low 11:23 0.9, High 17:35 5.0 and Low 23:49 1.0. The list covers 2026-10-17 to 2026-10-23, and 10-20 has only 3 tides.
     - JS helpers: `pad(n)`, `localDateKey(d)`, `toMinutes(hhmm)` and `render()`.
     - `render()` sets the date with `toLocaleDateString("en-GB", {weekday, day, month, year})` and clears the container.
       - If today isn't in the list, it shows a `<p class="empty">` naming the date range covered.
       - Otherwise it builds a table: thead "Tide / Time / Height". Each row has type + " water", time, and `height.toFixed(1) + " m"`.
       - Row logic is now `if (mins >= nowMins && !nextFound) { row.className = "next"; nextFound = true; }`. The next row gets a `<span class="tag">← Next</span>`.

4. Errors and fixes:
   - None. The user's feedback led me to remove the grey past-row styling, the blue header, the coloured backgrounds, shadows and rounded corners.

5. Problem Solving:
   - Porthmellow appears to be fictional, so the data is invented and labelled not for navigation.
   - I confirmed the page has no external dependencies, so it works offline.
   - I restyled the page for high contrast in bright sunlight. The next tide is now marked with bold text, thick borders and "← NEXT" instead of colour.

6. All user messages:
   - "Build a single page called tides.html that shows today's tide times for Porthmellow harbour from a hard-coded list. No frameworks and no build step."
   - "Never load anything from a CDN. The harbour office has no internet most days."
   - "I thought about a dark theme and I've ruled it out. The page gets read on a boat in bright sun, so keep it high contrast, dark text on white, nothing grey and nothing clever."

7. Pending Tasks:
   - Tell the user about the completed high-contrast restyle.

8. Current Work:
   I had just finished three edits to tides.html for the high-contrast request:
   - replaced the style block with black-on-white only;
   - removed the `row.className = "past"` branch;
   - changed the tag text to "← Next".

   All edits succeeded, but I haven't reported them to the user yet, and the page hasn't been tested in a browser.

9. Optional Next Step:
   Tell the user what changed for "keep it high contrast, dark text on white, nothing grey and nothing clever":
   - pure black on white;
   - larger text;
   - no grey for past tides;
   - the next tide marked with bold text, thick black borders and "← NEXT";
   - still no external resources.

   Note that the page hasn't been tested in a browser.

